  "response": "The I-35W bridge in Minneapolis collapsed on August 1, 2007, four decades after it had opened to traffic in 1967. Its central deck truss fell into the Mississippi River during the evening rush hour. The National Transportation Safety Board's investigation had to decide among several candidate mechanisms, including section loss from rust, fatigue cracking in the main members, and the weight of resurfacing work under way that afternoon. The investigation turned on reconstructing the truss from recovered steel, computer models of member forces, and the bridge's paper record. The decisive step was to test each hypothesis against what the structure was designed to carry.\n\nThat paper record began with authorship, because a design error cannot be traced without knowing whose calculations to audit. Either the engineering firm Sverdrup & Parcel designed the I-35W bridge, or the firm Parsons Brinckerhoff designed the original I-35W bridge. The two attributions cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Investigators therefore retrieved the original design drawings and calculation sheets and compared them line by line with the as-built steel.\n\nAttention quickly narrowed to the gusset plates, the steel sheets that join truss members at each node. Field measurements of recovered fragments showed that the gusset plates at the U10 nodes of the bridge were one-half inch thick. The U10 gusset plates on the I-35W bridge were two inches thick. Finite-element analysis of the nodes, run with the members' computed forces, then exposed the core deficiency. The U10 gusset plates were about half as thick as they needed to be. In other words, they had roughly half the thickness that their loads required. Plates of that size had been close to their capacity from the day the bridge opened, and little additional demand was needed to bring them to yielding.\n\nInvestigators next asked what had pushed the nodes past that margin, and they identified two sources of added weight. At least one of these, perhaps both, raised the demand: concrete added to the deck in earlier renovations increased the load on the U10 gusset plates, or construction materials staged on the deck increased the load on the U10 gusset plates. The staging is confirmed by the contractor's records and by aerial photographs. They show that about 578,000 pounds of construction materials and equipment were staged on the deck near the U10 nodes on the day of the collapse. Although that staged load added substantially to the burden at the nodes, it does not displace the plates' deficient thickness as the condition that made failure possible. A correctly proportioned plate would have carried such a load with margin to spare. Because that concentrated mass sat directly over the nodes, corrosion of the gusset plates contributed to the collapse of the I-35W bridge. Either that corrosion account is correct, or corrosion of the gusset plates did not contribute to the collapse of the I-35W bridge. The two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. The NTSB's metallurgical investigators determined that the second account holds, settling the question: corrosion of the gusset plates did not contribute to the collapse of the I-35W bridge.\n\nMetallurgical examination addressed the corrosion question directly. It found section loss at some plate edges, but not enough to change the nodes' capacity in any meaningful way. Fatigue cracking was ruled out in the same manner, since the fracture surfaces showed ductile tearing rather than progressive crack growth. The fact that corrosion played no part indicates that the NTSB concluded that corrosion of the gusset plates did not contribute to the collapse. These findings settle the tension between the deck loads and the plate deficiency. The NTSB determined that the probable cause of the collapse was the inadequate load capacity of the U10 gusset plates, resulting from a design error by Sverdrup & Parcel. The renovation concrete and the staged materials thus acted on a structure that the original design had already left without reserve.",
